WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.190
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:09.230
This is our 200th episode, so I know you're
expecting something great.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.690
See how you like this one!

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.589
One of the most sought after effect in light
transport simulations is capturing indirect

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:16.589
illumination.

00:00:16.589 --> 00:00:21.980
This is a beautiful effect where the color
of multiple diffuse, matte surfaces bleed

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.140
onto each other.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.370
And of course, computing such an effect is
as costly as it is beautiful because it requires

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:32.170
following the path of millions and millions
of light rays.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.310
This usually means several hours of waiting
time.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.500
There have been countless research papers
written on how to do this in real time, but

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.100
the limitations were often much too crippling
for practical use.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.210
But this time around, you will see soon that
these results are just outstanding, and we

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.510
will have a word on limitations at the end
of this video.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.079
The key contribution of this work is that
instead of computing the light transport between

00:00:56.079 --> 00:01:02.329
all possible point pairs in the scene, it
uses radiance probes that measure the nearby

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:07.250
illumination, and tries to reconstruct the
missing information from this sparse set of

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.710
radiance probes.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.000
After that, we place a bunch of receiver points
around the scene to places where we would

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.010
like to know how the indirect illumination
looks.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:20.410
There are several things to be taken care
of in the implementation of this idea.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:26.020
For instance, in previous works, the hierarchy
of these sender and receiver points was typically

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.020
fixed.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.140
In this new work, it is shown that a much
sparser set of carefully placed radiance probes

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.990
is sufficient to create high-quality reconstructions.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:40.479
This seemingly small difference also gives
rise to a lot of ambiguous cases that the

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:43.510
researchers needed to work out how to deal
with.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:48.110
For instance, possible occlusions between
the probes and receiver points need special

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.110
care.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.860
The entire algorithm is explained in a remarkably
intuitive way in the paper, make sure to have

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:54.900
a look at that.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.030
And, given that we can create images by performing
much less computation, with this technique,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.330
we can perform real-time light simulations.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:09.250
As you can see, 3.9 milliseconds is a typical
value for computing an entire image, which

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:14.359
means that this can be done with over 250
frames per second.

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:19.279
That's not only real time, that's several
times real time, if you will.

00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:20.279
Outstanding!

00:02:20.279 --> 00:02:24.599
And of course, now that we know that this
technique is fast, the next question is: how

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:26.209
accurate is it?

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:30.989
As expected, the outputs are always compared
to the reference footage so we can see how

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:33.090
accurate the proposed technique is.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.680
Clearly, there are differences, however, probably
many of us would fail to notice that we're

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.930
not looking at the reference footage, especially
if we don't have access to it, which is the

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.959
case in most applications.

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:49.599
And note that normally, we would have to wait
for hours for results like this.

00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:50.599
Isn't this incredible?

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:56.029
There are also tons of more comparisons in
the paper, for instance, it is also shown

00:02:56.029 --> 00:03:01.279
how the density of radiance probes relates
to the output quality and where the possible

00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:03.760
sweet spots are for industry practitioners.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.430
It is also tested against many competing solutions.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:14.209
Not only the results, but the number and quality
of comparisons is also top tier in this paper.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:18.730
However, like with all research works, no
new idea comes without limitations.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:24.469
This method works extremely well for static
scenes where not a lot of objects move around.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:28.699
Some movement is still fine as it is shown
in the video here, but drastic changes to

00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:34.510
the structure of the scene, like a large opening
door that remains unaccounted for by the probes

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.339
will lead to dips in the quality of the reconstruction.

00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:41.189
I think this is an excellent direction for
future research works.

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:45.349
If you enjoyed this episode, make sure to
subscribe and click the bell icon, we have

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:48.909
some more amazing papers coming up, you don't
want to miss that.

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:53.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

